## The question

EJ asks two linked questions:

1. **Does Claude (and today's other coding agents: Codex, Gemini/agy, opencode) already know by itself when and how to use memory well** — when to read it, when to write it, what to keep, what to leave out — or does it do this poorly unless told?
2. **So, should EJ's workflow-design method be enhanced with details of how agents use memory** in a designed workflow? EJ's view: *"as everyone said llms most important factors are two: memory and information."*

**Read the current research report first:** `/home/smoke01/dev/ancient-games/runs/20261013-memory-in-workflow-design/report.md`. It holds the evidence gathered so far (sources, quotes, what is verified and what is only reported) and what earlier experts said. Build on it: agree, disagree, or add what it misses. Do not repeat what it already says; name the report section you are responding to.

**Facts you can rely on:**
